(>> Solution to Question 4:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Distinguish between phase velocity and group velocity of wave packets. Prove mathematically that the group velocity of a de Broglie wave packet equals the classical particle velocity.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Definitions:) Tj
T*
(   - Phase Velocity \(v_p\): The velocity at which an individual monochromatic wavefront of constant) Tj
T*
(   phase advances through space:) Tj
T*
(   v_p = omega / k = \(2 * pi * nu\) / \(2 * pi / lambda\) = nu * lambda.) Tj
T*
(   - Group Velocity \(v_g\): The velocity at which the overall envelope \(modulation packet\)) Tj
T*
(   enclosing a group of interfering waves propagates:) Tj
T*
(   v_g = d\(omega\) / d\(k\).) Tj
T*
(   - A localized material particle cannot be represented by a single infinite plane wave \(which) Tj
T*
(   would have infinite uncertainty in position\). It must be represented by a localized wave packet) Tj
T*
(   formed by the superposition of waves spanning a band of frequencies and wavevectors.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Mathematical Proof for Non-Relativistic Particle:) Tj
T*
(   - By de Broglie and Planck relations:) Tj
T*
(   E = hbar * omega => omega = E / hbar) Tj
T*
(   p = hbar * k => k = p / hbar.) Tj
T*
(   - For a non-relativistic particle of mass m, kinetic energy is E = p^2 / \(2 * m\).) Tj
T*
(   - Substituting into the omega expression:) Tj
T*
(   omega = \(hbar^2 * k^2\) / \(2 * m * hbar\) = \(hbar * k^2\) / \(2 * m\).) Tj
T*
(   - Differentiating omega with respect to k to obtain group velocity:) Tj
T*
(   v_g = d\(omega\) / d\(k\) = d/dk [ \(hbar * k^2\) / \(2 * m\) ] = \(2 * hbar * k\) / \(2 * m\) = \(hbar * k\)) Tj
T*
(   / m.) Tj
T*
(   - Since p = hbar * k = m * v:) Tj
T*
(   v_g = p / m = \(m * v\) / m = v \(Classical Particle Velocity\). \(Q.E.D.\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Comparison with Phase Velocity:) Tj
T*
(   - Phase velocity: v_p = omega / k = [ \(hbar * k^2\) / \(2*m\) ] / k = \(hbar * k\) / \(2*m\) = v / 2.) Tj
T*
(   - The individual phase ripples inside the envelope move at half the speed of the particle,) Tj
T*
(   while the wave packet envelope travels at exactly the classical velocity v, carrying the) Tj
T*
(   particle's energy and momentum.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 5:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: State Heisenberg's Uncertainty Principle for position-momentum and energy-time. Explain why an electron cannot exist inside an atomic nucleus.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Statement of Heisenberg's Uncertainty Principle \(1927\):) Tj
T*
(   - It is physically impossible to simultaneously measure both the exact position and exact) Tj
T*
(   conjugate momentum of a quantum particle with arbitrary precision.) Tj
T*
(   - If Delta_x is the uncertainty in position along the x-axis and Delta_p_x is the uncertainty) Tj
T*
(   in the x-component of linear momentum, their product is bounded by:) Tj
T*
(   Delta_x * Delta_p_x >= hbar / 2, where hbar = h / \(2 * pi\) = 1.0546 x 10^-34 J*s.) Tj
T*
(   - Similarly, for energy and time:) Tj
T*
(   Delta_E * Delta_t >= hbar / 2,) Tj
T*
(   meaning an energy state that exists for a finite lifetime Delta_t possesses an inherent energy) Tj
T*
(   uncertainty Delta_E.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Proof: Non-Existence of Free Electrons in Atomic Nucleus:) Tj
T*
ET

