45 780 Td
(   - Let an electron be hypothesized to reside permanently inside an atomic nucleus.) Tj
T*
(   - Typical nuclear diameter: 2 * R_nuc ~= 10^-14 m. Therefore, the maximum uncertainty in the) Tj
T*
(   electron's position is:) Tj
T*
(   Delta_x ~= 1.0 x 10^-14 m.) Tj
T*
(   - By Heisenberg's uncertainty principle, the minimum uncertainty in electron momentum is:) Tj
T*
(   Delta_p >= hbar / \(2 * Delta_x\) = \(1.055 x 10^-34 J*s\) / \(2 * 1.0 x 10^-14 m\) = 5.275 x 10^-21) Tj
T*
(   kg*m/s.) Tj
T*
(   - The actual momentum p of the electron must be at least comparable to its uncertainty: p_min) Tj
T*
(   ~= 5.275 x 10^-21 kg*m/s.) Tj
T*
(   - Since p*c = \(5.275 x 10^-21\) * \(3.0 x 10^8\) = 1.58 x 10^-12 J >> m_0*c^2 \(8.2 x 10^-14 J\),) Tj
T*
(   the electron is ultra-relativistic: E ~= p * c.) Tj
T*
(   - Converting energy to electron-volts:) Tj
T*
(   E_min = \(1.58 x 10^-12 J\) / \(1.602 x 10^-19 J/eV\) ~= 9.88 x 10^6 eV ~= 10 MeV.) Tj
T*
(   - Experimental Nuclear Fact: Beta-decay electrons emitted by nuclei have energies rarely) Tj
T*
(   exceeding 2 to 3 MeV. Furthermore, nuclear attractive potential wells for electrons are only ~) Tj
T*
(   1 MeV deep.) Tj
T*
(   - A 10 MeV electron cannot be bound by nuclear forces and would escape instantly. Therefore,) Tj
T*
(   electrons cannot exist as permanent constituent particles inside atomic nuclei \(beta rays are) Tj
T*
(   created at the instant of neutron decay\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 6:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the physical interpretation of the quantum wave function Psi\(x,t\). State Born's statistical postulate and list the mathematical conditions for an acceptable wave function.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Nature of the Wave Function Psi\(x,t\):) Tj
T*
(   - In quantum mechanics, the complete dynamical state of a particle is described by a) Tj
T*
(   complex-valued scalar function Psi\(x,y,z,t\).) Tj
T*
(   - Unlike classical water or sound waves, Psi has no direct classical physical reality; it is a) Tj
T*
(   complex mathematical probability amplitude.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Born's Statistical Postulate \(Max Born, 1926\):) Tj
T*
(   - The physical reality of the wave function lies in its squared absolute magnitude:) Tj
T*
(   P\(x,t\) = |Psi\(x,t\)|^2 = Psi*\(x,t\) * Psi\(x,t\),) Tj
T*
(   where Psi* is the complex conjugate of Psi.) Tj
T*
(   - P\(x,t\) represents the Probability Density. The probability dP of finding the particle within) Tj
T*
(   an infinitesimal 1D volume dx around position x at time t is:) Tj
T*
(   dP = |Psi\(x,t\)|^2 dx.) Tj
T*
(   - Since the particle must exist somewhere in the universe with 100% certainty, the wave) Tj
T*
(   function must satisfy the Normalization Condition:) Tj
T*
(   Integral_{-inf}^{+inf} |Psi\(x,t\)|^2 dx = 1.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Mandatory Boundary Conditions for a Well-Behaved Wave Function:) Tj
T*
(   - \(a\) Single-Valued: Psi\(x\) must have only one unique value at any given spatial point x,) Tj
T*
(   because probability at a point cannot be multi-valued.) Tj
T*
(   - \(b\) Continuous: Psi\(x\) must be mathematically continuous everywhere with no finite jumps,) Tj
T*
(   ensuring continuous probability.) Tj
T*
(   - \(c\) Continuous First Derivative: dPsi / dx must be continuous everywhere \(except at points) Tj
T*
(   where potential V becomes infinite\), ensuring momentum p = -i*hbar*\(d/dx\) is well-defined.) Tj
T*
(   - \(d\) Square-Integrable \(Normalizable\): The integral of |Psi|^2 over all space must be finite.) Tj
T*
(   Consequently, Psi\(x\) must tend asymptotically to zero as x -> +- infinity.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

endstream
