BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the physical significance of Zero-Point Energy in quantum systems. Why does classical mechanics predict E_ground = 0 while quantum mechanics demands E1 > 0?) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Concept of Zero-Point Energy:) Tj
T*
(   - Zero-point energy is the minimum possible energy that a quantum mechanical physical system) Tj
T*
(   may possess in its ground state at absolute zero temperature \(T = 0 K\).) Tj
T*
(   - For a particle in an infinite well of width L: E1 = h^2 / \(8 * m * L^2\) > 0.) Tj
T*
(   - For a quantum harmonic oscillator: E0 = 0.5 * hbar * omega > 0.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Classical Mechanics Failure \(E_ground = 0\):) Tj
T*
(   - In classical Newtonian mechanics, absolute zero temperature corresponds to complete cessation) Tj
T*
(   of all atomic motion.) Tj
T*
(   - A classical particle inside a box can sit completely motionless at rest \(velocity v = 0,) Tj
T*
(   momentum p = 0\) at the bottom of the well \(potential V = 0\), yielding total energy E = 0.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Quantum Mechanical Demand for E1 > 0 via Uncertainty Principle:) Tj
T*
(   - In quantum mechanics, confining a particle inside a finite spatial region of width L limits) Tj
T*
(   its position uncertainty to Delta_x <= L.) Tj
T*
(   - According to Heisenberg's uncertainty principle: Delta_x * Delta_p >= hbar / 2.) Tj
T*
(   - Therefore, the particle must possess an irreducible momentum uncertainty:) Tj
T*
(   Delta_p >= hbar / \(2 * L\).) Tj
T*
(   - If total kinetic energy were zero \(E = 0\), both momentum and position would have to be known) Tj
T*
(   with absolute zero uncertainty \(p = 0 and Delta_p = 0\), violating the uncertainty principle.) Tj
T*
(   - Hence, quantum particles are fundamentally prohibited from ever coming to rest; an) Tj
T*
(   inescapable quantum jiggle \(zero-point fluctuation\) persists eternally even at absolute zero.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Physical Manifestations in Condensed Matter:) Tj
T*
(   - Liquid Helium: Helium remains a liquid at 1 atmosphere down to absolute zero \(never freezes) Tj
T*
(   into a solid\) purely because its light atomic mass creates enormous zero-point vibrations that) Tj
T*
(   disrupt crystal lattice formation.) Tj
T*
ET

