(   [Problem Statement]: Calculate the minimum uncertainty in the velocity of: \(a\) an electron confined to a 0.05 nm atom, \(b\) a 0.20 kg concrete core test cylinder positioned with precision 1.0 micrometer.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Governing Heisenberg Uncertainty Relation:) Tj
T*
(   - Delta_x * Delta_p >= hbar / 2 => Delta_p >= hbar / \(2 * Delta_x\).) Tj
T*
(   - Since p = m * v: Delta_p = m * Delta_v => Delta_v >= hbar / \(2 * m * Delta_x\).) Tj
T*
(   - Constant: hbar / 2 = \(1.0546 x 10^-34 J*s\) / 2 = 5.273 x 10^-35 J*s.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. \(a\) For Confined Electron \(m_e = 9.109 x 10^-31 kg, Delta_x = 0.05 nm = 5.0 x 10^-11 m\):) Tj
T*
(   - Delta_v >= \(5.273 x 10^-35\) / [ 9.109 x 10^-31 * 5.0 x 10^-11 ]) Tj
T*
(   - Denominator = 4.555 x 10^-41 kg*m.) Tj
T*
(   - Delta_v >= \(5.273 x 10^-35\) / \(4.555 x 10^-41\) = 1.158 x 10^6 m/s.) Tj
T*
(   - Velocity uncertainty is immense \(~ 1158 km/s, over 0.3% the speed of light!\). Quantum effects) Tj
T*
(   govern atomic dynamics.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. \(b\) For Concrete Cylinder \(m = 0.20 kg, Delta_x = 1.0 micrometer = 1.0 x 10^-6 m\):) Tj
T*
(   - Delta_v >= \(5.273 x 10^-35\) / [ 0.20 * 1.0 x 10^-6 ] = \(5.273 x 10^-35\) / \(2.0 x 10^-7\) =) Tj
T*
(   2.64 x 10^-28 m/s.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Engineering Comparison:) Tj
T*
(   - A velocity uncertainty of 10^-28 m/s means the concrete cylinder would take over 10^20 years) Tj
T*
(   to shift by the width of a single atom!) Tj
T*
(   - Classical deterministic Newtonian mechanics applies flawlessly to civil engineering) Tj
T*
(   structures.) Tj
T*
() Tj
