(   [Problem Statement]: An electron with energy E = 3.0 eV approaches a rectangular potential barrier of height V_0 = 7.0 eV and width w = 0.40 nm. Calculate: \(a\) wave attenuation constant alpha, \(b\) tunneling transmission coefficient T, \(c\) transmission coefficient if barrier width is doubled to 0.80 nm.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Given Parameters:) Tj
T*
(   - Electron mass m_e = 9.109 x 10^-31 kg; hbar = 1.0546 x 10^-34 J*s.) Tj
T*
(   - Particle energy E = 3.0 eV; Barrier height V_0 = 7.0 eV.) Tj
T*
(   - Barrier potential difference: V_0 - E = 7.0 - 3.0 = 4.0 eV = 4.0 * 1.602 x 10^-19 J = 6.408 x) Tj
T*
(   10^-19 J.) Tj
T*
(   - Barrier width w1 = 0.40 nm = 4.0 x 10^-10 m; w2 = 0.80 nm = 8.0 x 10^-10 m.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. \(a\) Wave Attenuation Constant alpha:) Tj
T*
(   - alpha = sqrt\( 2 * m_e * \(V_0 - E\) \) / hbar) Tj
T*
(   - alpha = sqrt\( 2 * 9.109 x 10^-31 * 6.408 x 10^-19 \) / \(1.0546 x 10^-34\)) Tj
T*
(   = sqrt\( 1.1674 x 10^-48 \) / \(1.0546 x 10^-34\) = \(1.0805 x 10^-24\) / \(1.0546 x 10^-34\) = 1.0245) Tj
T*
(   x 10^10 m^-1 = 10.25 nm^-1.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. \(b\) Transmission Coefficient T1 for w = 0.40 nm:) Tj
T*
(   - Attenuation exponent: 2 * alpha * w1 = 2 * \(10.245 nm^-1\) * \(0.40 nm\) = 8.196.) Tj
T*
(   - Pre-exponential factor: P_0 = 16 * \(E / V_0\) * \( 1 - E / V_0 \) = 16 * \(3 / 7\) * \(1 - 3/7\) =) Tj
T*
(   16 * \(3/7\) * \(4/7\) = 192 / 49 = 3.918.) Tj
T*
(   - T1 = P_0 * exp\( - 2 * alpha * w1 \) = 3.918 * exp\( - 8.196 \) = 3.918 * \(2.757 x 10^-4\) = 1.08) Tj
T*
(   x 10^-3 = 0.108%.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. \(c\) Transmission Coefficient T2 for Doubled Width w = 0.80 nm:) Tj
T*
(   - Attenuation exponent: 2 * alpha * w2 = 2 * 8.196 = 16.392.) Tj
T*
(   - T2 = 3.918 * exp\( - 16.392 \) = 3.918 * \(7.604 x 10^-8\) = 2.98 x 10^-7 = 0.0000298%.) Tj
T*
(   - Doubling the barrier width causes transmission to plummet by a factor of 3,600!) Tj
T*
ET

