BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the correspondence principle proposed by Niels Bohr. Demonstrate how the quantum energy eigenvalues and probability distributions for a particle in a box converge to classical physics for large quantum numbers \(n -> infinity\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Statement of Bohr's Correspondence Principle \(1923\):) Tj
T*
(   - Quantum mechanics must reproduce the predictions of classical physics in the limit of large) Tj
T*
(   quantum numbers \(n -> infinity\) or when the action is macroscopic compared to Planck's constant) Tj
T*
(   \(h -> 0\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Convergence of Energy Spacing:) Tj
T*
(   - Quantized energy levels in a 1D box: E_n = \(n^2 * h^2\) / \(8 * m * L^2\).) Tj
T*
(   - The energy difference between adjacent levels:) Tj
T*
(   Delta_E = E_{n+1} - E_n = [ \(n+1\)^2 - n^2 ] * E1 = \(2*n + 1\) * E1.) Tj
T*
(   - Relative fractional energy gap between levels:) Tj
T*
(   Delta_E / E_n = [ \(2*n + 1\) * E1 ] / \(n^2 * E1\) ~= 2 / n.) Tj
T*
(   - As n -> infinity: lim_{n->inf} \(Delta_E / E_n\) = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - The discrete energy spectrum merges into an apparently continuous spectrum indistinguishable) Tj
T*
(   from classical mechanics.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Convergence of Spatial Probability Distribution:) Tj
T*
(   - Classical probability distribution: A classical particle bouncing elastically between walls) Tj
T*
(   at constant speed spends equal time in every unit length dx:) Tj
T*
(   P_classical\(x\) = 1 / L = constant.) Tj
T*
(   - Quantum probability distribution: P_n\(x\) = \(2 / L\) * sin^2\( n * pi * x / L \).) Tj
T*
(   - For large n, the spatial oscillation frequency of sin^2 becomes extremely rapid.) Tj
T*
(   - Any macroscopic instrument averages over an observation window Delta_x containing thousands) Tj
T*
(   of oscillations.) Tj
T*
(   - The average of sin^2\(theta\) over many cycles is exactly 1/2:) Tj
T*
(   <P_n\(x\)> = \(2 / L\) * \(1/2\) = 1 / L = P_classical\(x\).) Tj
T*
(   - Quantum fluctuations wash out macroscopically, confirming classical physics.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 21:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: A quantum harmonic oscillator has mass m = 1.0 x 10^-26 kg and spring force constant k = 100 N/m. Calculate: \(a\) classical angular frequency omega, \(b\) zero-point energy E0 in eV, \(c\) energy of emitted photon during transition from n = 3 to n = 2.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Given Data:) Tj
T*
(   - Oscillator mass m = 1.0 x 10^-26 kg.) Tj
T*
(   - Force constant k = 100 N/m.) Tj
T*
(   - hbar = 1.0546 x 10^-34 J*s; 1 eV = 1.602 x 10^-19 J.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. \(a\) Angular Frequency omega:) Tj
T*
(   - omega = sqrt\( k / m \) = sqrt\( 100 / \(1.0 x 10^-26\) \) = sqrt\( 1.0 x 10^28 \) = 1.0 x 10^14) Tj
T*
(   rad/s.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. \(b\) Zero-Point Energy E0:) Tj
T*
(   - Energy levels of quantum harmonic oscillator: E_n = \(n + 1/2\) * hbar * omega, for n = 0, 1,) Tj
T*
(   2, ...) Tj
T*
(   - Ground state zero-point energy \(n = 0\):) Tj
T*
(   E0 = 0.5 * hbar * omega = 0.5 * \(1.0546 x 10^-34 J*s\) * \(1.0 x 10^14 s^-1\) = 5.273 x 10^-21) Tj
T*
ET

